# Project Storyboard Package

Use this reference when the user wants to organize approved images into professional storyboard boards for a real estate, architecture, archviz, interiors, exterior, or property launch project.

The goal is to evolve beyond isolated beautiful images. Build a repeatable project presentation system that combines cinematic direction, architectural intent, technical detailing, material logic, and video storytelling.

## Core Idea

After the world bible, prompts, and generated images are approved, create a storyboard package for the project.

The package should include:

- individual boards for each approved image, room, facade, aerial, amenity, detail, or lifestyle scene;
- architectural detail boards explaining relevant design/construction decisions;
- moodboard sections for atmosphere, materiality, light, camera, and lifestyle;
- a 4 to 6 frame film-sequence board that tells the story of the video like a director;
- video prompts and motion notes derived from each approved board;
- revision notes to improve the next generation round.

## Package Structure

Use this structure:

```text
01-project-overview
02-world-bible-summary
03-reference-dna
04-approved-image-index
05-space-storyboards
06-architectural-detail-boards
07-film-sequence-storyboards
08-video-prompts
09-revision-and-continuity-notes
```

## Board Types

### Project Overview Board

Purpose: orient the client/team before individual scenes.

Include:

- project name;
- world/campaign name;
- typology;
- target audience;
- emotional promise;
- architectural concept;
- core materials;
- light/climate logic;
- camera language;
- approved visual anchors.

### Moodboard / Visual DNA Board

Purpose: lock the visual direction.

Include:

- atmosphere;
- color palette;
- material palette;
- lighting references;
- landscape/vegetation logic;
- human/lifestyle tone;
- camera/lens language;
- no-go visual cliches.

### Individual Scene Board

Purpose: document one approved image or planned image.

Include:

- board ID;
- scene name;
- approved image or image placeholder;
- scene role in the campaign/video;
- mood and emotional function;
- architecture notes;
- material notes;
- lighting/time of day;
- camera/lens/composition;
- motion direction for video;
- human action, if any;
- technical detailing opportunities;
- continuity anchors;
- what must not change;
- image prompt;
- video prompt;
- revision notes.

### Room / Space Storyboard

Purpose: explain one environment deeply.

Use for:

- living room;
- kitchen;
- suite;
- bathroom/lavabo;
- lobby;
- rooftop;
- pool;
- terrace;
- facade;
- aerial view;
- garden;
- amenity.

Include:

- function of the space;
- spatial relationship to adjacent spaces;
- focal wall or key view;
- materials and finishes;
- furniture/staging logic;
- light and shadow behavior;
- detailing boards needed;
- suggested stills;
- suggested video movements.

### Architectural Detail Board

Purpose: translate the visual into professional architecture/documentation logic.

Use the detailing menu in `architectural-detailing-menu.md` to choose relevant topics.

Include:

- detail title;
- where it appears in the approved image;
- why it matters for the project;
- technical/documentation language;
- material transition;
- alignment/pagination logic;
- lighting/electrical/forro/hydraulic/paisagismo implications when relevant;
- prompt language to preserve the detail in AI generation;
- caution notes for realism.

### Film Sequence Board

Purpose: tell the story of the video with 4 to 6 frames.

Default six-beat structure:

1. Establishing shot: site, city, landscape, facade, or aerial reveal.
2. Arrival: entrance, path, lobby, gate, porte-cochere, or threshold.
3. Spatial reveal: living, terrace, amenity, room, or main architectural volume.
4. Detail beat: material, hand gesture, light, water, marcenaria, stone, facade transition.
5. Lifestyle proof: realistic human use of the property.
6. Emotional close: blue hour facade, skyline, view, pool, garden, or quiet final frame.

Each frame must include:

- frame number;
- image/scene;
- narrative purpose;
- camera movement;
- duration suggestion;
- transition;
- architectural focus;
- emotional beat;
- video prompt.

## Professional Quality Rules

- Every board needs a purpose: selling, explaining, sequencing, detailing, or preserving continuity.
- Do not create decorative boards with no project function.
- The property is the hero, even when people appear.
- Keep technical language clear enough for architecture/interior professionals but not overloaded.
- Separate what is approved from what is proposed.
- Use images as evidence and annotations as decisions.
- Every storyboard must say what to preserve in the next generation.
- Every film sequence must have beginning, progression, and closure.
- Avoid generic labels like "luxury mood"; name the actual material, light, space, and emotion.

## Output Format In Chat

When no visual layout file is requested, deliver boards in structured Markdown:

```markdown
## Board 01 - [Scene Name]

Purpose:

Approved image:

Mood:

Architectural notes:

Material notes:

Lighting:

Camera:

Motion:

Technical details to document:

Continuity anchors:

Do not change:

Image prompt:

Video prompt:

Revision notes:
```

When the user wants actual generated storyboard images, create prompts for board images that include the approved image, labels, annotations, material callouts, shot numbers, and a clean professional architecture presentation layout.

## Storyboard Image Prompt Template

```text
Create a professional architecture storyboard board for [PROJECT / WORLD NAME], focused on [SCENE / SPACE]. Use the approved image as the main visual anchor. Add clean editorial layout, architectural callouts, material palette swatches, mood notes, camera direction, lighting notes, and technical detail labels. Keep the design elegant, restrained, presentation-ready for a high-end real estate or architecture client. Do not alter the architecture from the approved image. Avoid fantasy styling, decorative clutter, fake technical drawings, unreadable tiny text, and over-designed graphics.
```

## Film Sequence Board Prompt Template

```text
Create a cinematic real estate film storyboard board for [PROJECT / WORLD NAME] with [4/5/6] frames arranged in a clean professional grid. Each frame represents a shot in the property video: [SHOT LIST]. Include shot numbers, concise scene names, camera movement notes, emotional beat, architectural focus, and transition notes. Maintain a premium architecture presentation style, realistic real estate tone, clear hierarchy, and consistent project identity. Do not invent architecture beyond the approved images. Avoid fantasy, anime, stock-photo styling, unreadable text, and over-rendered graphics.
```

## Iteration Loop

After each storyboard package, ask implicitly through the output:

- Which boards are approved?
- Which spaces need more detail?
- Which images need regeneration?
- Which details should become permanent project rules?
- Which board format should become the user's preferred template?
